{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Yes.** For a RIC already authorized to operate ETF and mutual-fund share classes sharing one portfolio, ETF-class in-kind redemptions can benefit taxable mutual-fund-class shareholders.\n\n**Mechanism:** The RIC delivers portfolio securities rather than cash to redeeming ETF authorized participants. Under IRC §852(b)(6), the RIC generally recognizes no gain on that distribution. Delivering appreciated, low-basis securities removes embedded gains from the shared portfolio, potentially reducing future capital-gain distributions to **both** classes.\n\n**Limits:** This does not erase previously realized gains, eliminate taxable investment income, or prevent gains from portfolio trading or cash redemptions. Benefits depend on available redemptions, permissible baskets, portfolio holdings, and operational constraints; they are not guaranteed. Shareholders still owe applicable taxes on distributions and their own share sales.\n\n**Worth knowing:** SEC permission for a multiclass ETF structure is separate from RIC tax treatment; it cannot be assumed for every fund."}}
